    const Section(
      title: 'Initial Presentation',
      content: 'A 5-year-old boy is referred to the pediatric PM&R spasticity clinic for progressive difficulty walking and increasing toe-walking. He was born at 28 weeks gestation weighing 1,100 grams, with a NICU course complicated by Grade II bilateral intraventricular hemorrhage and periventricular leukomalacia seen on head ultrasound. He was diagnosed with spastic diplegic cerebral palsy at 18 months when he failed to achieve independent sitting. He achieved independent ambulation at age 3.5 years using a posterior walker. He currently walks with bilateral hinged AFOs and a posterior walker at home but uses a manual wheelchair for community distances. His parents report worsening toe-walking over the past 6 months with increasing difficulty getting his heels to the ground, frequent tripping, and new scissoring pattern. He receives school-based PT twice weekly and private outpatient PT once weekly. His last botulinum toxin injections to bilateral gastrocnemius muscles were 8 months ago with diminishing benefit compared to prior injections. He is cognitively age-appropriate and attends a regular kindergarten classroom. His father is a physical therapist at another facility and has been researching selective dorsal rhizotomy (SDR). His mother is a stay-at-home parent who is anxious about surgical intervention. The parents are in significant conflict about the treatment approach.',
