    const Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content: '**Cerebral Palsy Classification**:\n- **Type**: Spastic diplegia (bilateral spastic CP predominantly affecting lower extremities)\n- **GMFCS Level**: Currently Level III (walks with hand-held mobility device indoors, uses wheeled mobility outdoors and in the community). Important to note: GMFCS level can appear to worsen during growth spurts even without true neurological deterioration.\n- **MACS (Manual Ability Classification System)**: Level I (handles objects easily and successfully)\n- **CFCS (Communication Function Classification System)**: Level I (effective sender and receiver with familiar and unfamiliar partners)\n\n**Spasticity Assessment**:\n- **Modified Ashworth Scale (MAS)**: Quantify tone at each joint (hip adductors, hamstrings, gastrocnemius/soleus, tibialis posterior)\n- **Modified Tardieu Scale**: More specific for spasticity — measures the angle of catch (R1) vs. passive ROM (R2). The difference (R2-R1) indicates the dynamic component amenable to spasticity treatment. If R1 is close to R2, the contracture is fixed.\n- **Key distinction**: Spasticity (velocity-dependent increase in tone) vs. dystonia (sustained or intermittent involuntary muscle contractions). Both can coexist in CP. Dystonia is not effectively treated with SDR or botulinum toxin targeting spasticity.\n\n**Growth and Development Concerns**:\n- Children with CP often experience functional decline during growth spurts because bone grows faster than spastic muscles, worsening contractures (the "birthday syndrome")\n- This patient\'s worsening equinus at age 5 is consistent with a growth-related decline\n- Puberty will bring another major period of risk\n\n**Current Treatment Review**:\n- Botulinum toxin injections with diminishing returns suggest either antibody formation, progressive contracture (fixed vs. dynamic), or dosing/targeting issues\n- PT 3x/week is appropriate but may need goal reassessment\n- AFO design should be reviewed — is the current AFO accommodating his equinus or correcting it?\n\n**Gait Analysis**:\n- Instrumented gait analysis (3D motion capture, EMG, force plates) is the gold standard for surgical decision-making in ambulatory CP\n- Should be performed before any orthopedic or neurosurgical intervention\n- Identifies specific gait deviations, muscle firing patterns, and which muscles are contributing to dysfunction\n\n**Comorbidity Screening**:\n- Hip surveillance: AP pelvis radiograph annually until skeletal maturity. Migration percentage >33% requires close monitoring. GMFCS III patients have 40% risk of hip displacement.\n- Spine: Monitor for scoliosis\n- Vision and hearing: Periventricular leukomalacia associated with cortical visual impairment\n- Nutritional status: Growth charts for CP (Brooks 2011)\n- Bone health: Increased fracture risk from decreased weight-bearing, consider DXA if prior fracture',
