      content: '**Vitals**: Weight 18 kg (25th percentile for CP), Height 107 cm (10th percentile). Well-appearing, cooperative, interactive boy.\n\n**Neurologic — Tone Assessment**:\n- **Hip adductors**: MAS 2 bilaterally, Modified Tardieu R1 = 20 degrees abduction (catch), R2 = 45 degrees (full passive ROM). Dynamic component present (R2-R1 = 25 degrees).\n- **Hamstrings**: MAS 1+ bilaterally, popliteal angle 45 degrees bilaterally (normal >60). Fixed component present.\n- **Gastrocnemius**: MAS 3 bilaterally, Modified Tardieu R1 = -10 degrees dorsiflexion with knee extended (catch at 10 degrees plantar flexion), R2 = 5 degrees dorsiflexion. Significant dynamic component but limited passive range.\n- **Soleus**: With knee flexed, dorsiflexion to 10 degrees passively. This indicates the gastrocnemius is the primary contributor to equinus (two-joint muscle tighter than single-joint soleus).\n- **Tibialis posterior**: Mild spasticity contributing to varus in swing phase\n- **No dystonia detected**: Tone is clearly velocity-dependent, increases with speed, and has a catch. No sustained posturing at rest.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**:\n- Bilateral dynamic equinus contractures (correctable to 5 degrees DF with knee flexed but not beyond -10 degrees with knee extended)\n- Mild bilateral hamstring tightness\n- Hip flexion contractures: Thomas test positive at 10 degrees bilaterally\n- Femoral anteversion: Internal rotation 60 degrees bilaterally (increased), external rotation 20 degrees (decreased) — lever arm dysfunction\n- No hip click or instability. Galeazzi sign negative.\n\n**Gait Observation**:\n- Bilateral equinus gait with forefoot initial contact\n- Scissoring (hip adductor spasticity) during swing phase\n- Crouched posture at the knees in midstance\n- Increased lumbar lordosis (hip flexor tightness compensation)\n- Requires bilateral handhold support, unable to walk without posterior walker\n- Falls 3-4 times daily per parent report\n\n**Functional Assessment**:\n- Gross Motor Function Measure (GMFM-66): 58% (consistent with GMFCS III)\n- Transfers: Sit-to-stand with pull-to-stand on furniture. Floor-to-stand requires furniture assist.\n- Stairs: Ascends and descends with one rail and one hand held, step-to pattern\n- Self-care: Independent in feeding, upper body dressing. Requires help with lower body dressing, toileting assistance for balance.\n\n**Imaging**: AP pelvis radiograph: bilateral Reimer migration percentage 22% (within normal limits, <33%). No subluxation.',
