    const Section(
      title: '+ Discuss SDR candidacy criteria, compare it to other spasticity management options, and interpret gait lab data in decision-making.',
      content: '**Selective Dorsal Rhizotomy (SDR) — Candidacy Criteria**:\nSDR involves selective sectioning of dorsal (sensory) nerve rootlets at L1-S1 that contribute most to spasticity, based on intraoperative EMG response to stimulation.\n\n**Ideal SDR Candidate**:\n- Spastic diplegia (bilateral spastic CP predominantly in lower extremities)\n- GMFCS Level II or III (ambulatory with or without device)\n- Age 4-8 years (before fixed contractures develop)\n- Predominantly spasticity (not dystonia or ataxia)\n- Good underlying strength (can generate voluntary movement when spasticity is temporarily reduced, e.g., after botulinum toxin)\n- Adequate selective motor control\n- Good cognitive function and ability to participate in intensive post-operative rehabilitation\n- Supportive family who can commit to 6-12 months of intensive post-operative therapy\n- No significant fixed contractures (contractures will not be corrected by SDR and may require orthopedic surgery)\n- Gait lab data showing spasticity as the primary driver of gait abnormality\n\n**This Patient\'s SDR Candidacy**:\n- Favorable: Spastic diplegia, GMFCS III, age 5, pure spasticity (no dystonia), good cognition, motivated family\n- Concerns: Developing fixed contracture at the ankle (R2 only 5 degrees DF with knee extended), hamstring tightness (may need concurrent or staged orthopedic surgery), GMFCS III (most data for SDR is in GMFCS II; outcomes in level III are more variable)\n- **Gait lab is essential before proceeding** — must confirm spasticity is the primary gait deviation driver and assess whether the crouch and equinus are primarily dynamic (spasticity) or structural (contracture/bony)\n\n**Comparison of Spasticity Management Options**:\n\n1. **Botulinum Toxin (BoNT-A)**:\n   - Focal, reversible, outpatient procedure\n   - Duration: 3-6 months per cycle\n   - Best for focal spasticity in 1-4 muscle groups\n   - This patient has diminishing benefit — possible reasons: antibody formation (consider switching from onabotulinumtoxinA to abobotulinumtoxinA), progressive contracture making spasticity treatment alone insufficient, or dosing issues\n   - Maximum dose: 16-20 units/kg body weight per session (depending on formulation)\n\n2. **Serial Casting**:\n   - Short-stretch fiberglass casts applied progressively to improve ROM\n   - Often combined with botulinum toxin (inject, then cast 1-2 weeks later)\n   - Can gain 10-15 degrees of dorsiflexion over 4-6 weeks of casting\n   - Good option to maximize ROM before considering SDR\n\n3. **Intrathecal Baclofen (ITB)**:\n   - Delivers baclofen directly to the spinal cord via implanted pump\n   - Best for diffuse bilateral spasticity in GMFCS IV-V\n   - Generally not first-line for ambulatory patients (risk of excessive weakness)\n   - Requires surgical implantation and refills every 3-6 months\n   - Complication risks: catheter malfunction, infection, withdrawal syndrome (medical emergency)\n\n4. **Oral Medications**:\n   - Baclofen, diazepam, tizanidine, dantrolene\n   - Systemic side effects (sedation, cognitive dulling) limit utility in children\n   - Generally adjunctive, not primary treatment in ambulatory CP\n\n5. **SDR**:\n   - Permanent, irreversible spasticity reduction\n   - Does NOT address contractures, bony deformities, or weakness\n   - Requires 6-12 months of intensive PT post-operatively (3-5 sessions per week)\n   - Risk of new weakness, sensory changes, bladder dysfunction (transient in most), spinal complications\n   - Long-term outcomes: 20-year follow-up studies show sustained spasticity reduction, but many patients still require orthopedic surgery later\n\n6. **Orthopedic Surgery (Single-Event Multilevel Surgery — SEMLS)**:\n   - Addresses fixed contractures and bony deformities in one surgical session\n   - May include: gastrocnemius recession or lengthening, hamstring lengthening, femoral derotation osteotomy, adductor release\n   - Should be guided by gait lab data\n   - Can be done instead of or in addition to SDR\n\n**Recommended Approach for This Patient**:\n1. Obtain instrumented gait analysis\n2. Trial of BoNT-A with serial casting to bilateral gastrocnemius (combined approach to maximize ROM)\n3. Based on gait lab results, discuss SDR vs. SEMLS vs. combined approach with the neurosurgery and orthopedic teams\n4. The gait lab data will determine whether spasticity alone (SDR) or spasticity plus contracture/bony problems (SEMLS +/- SDR) is the best strategy',
