    const Section(
      title: '+ How do you differentiate spasticity from dystonia in this patient, and why does it matter for treatment selection?',
      content: '**Spasticity vs. Dystonia — Clinical Differentiation**:\n\n**Spasticity**:\n- Velocity-dependent increase in tonic stretch reflexes\n- Has a "catch" at a specific angle (Modified Tardieu R1)\n- Increases with faster passive stretch\n- Clasp-knife quality (resistance then sudden release)\n- Worse with anxiety, cold, pain\n- Diminishes during sleep\n- Involves antigravity muscles preferentially\n- Upper motor neuron sign (hyperreflexia, clonus, Babinski)\n\n**Dystonia**:\n- Sustained or intermittent involuntary muscle contractions causing abnormal postures or repetitive movements\n- NOT velocity-dependent — present regardless of speed of passive movement\n- May be triggered by voluntary movement (action dystonia) or present at rest\n- Can involve agonist and antagonist muscles simultaneously (co-contraction)\n- May have a directional preference (twisting, turning)\n- Can worsen with fatigue, stress, and voluntary effort\n- May improve with sensory tricks (geste antagoniste)\n- Does NOT have the clasp-knife quality\n\n**Why It Matters**:\n- **SDR only treats spasticity** — it sections sensory rootlets that drive the spastic reflex arc. If the predominant movement disorder is dystonia, SDR will unmask weakness without addressing the abnormal posturing.\n- **Botulinum toxin treats both** spasticity and dystonia but through different mechanisms\n- **ITB can treat both** but is more effective for spasticity\n- **Oral baclofen**: Primarily anti-spasticity\n- **Trihexyphenidyl (Artane)**: Anti-dystonia medication (anticholinergic). Would not help pure spasticity.\n- **Deep brain stimulation**: For severe dystonia, not spasticity\n\n**Assessment in This Patient**:\n- The velocity-dependent catch on Modified Tardieu testing\n- The presence of clonus and hyperreflexia\n- The clasp-knife quality of resistance\n- The absence of sustained posturing at rest\n- All confirm that spasticity, not dystonia, is the predominant movement disorder, making him a reasonable SDR candidate from a neurological standpoint\n\n**Hypertonia Assessment Tool (HAT)**:\n- Validated tool to differentiate spasticity, dystonia, and rigidity in children with CP\n- Particularly useful when both spasticity and dystonia coexist (mixed CP)\n- Should be administered by experienced clinicians',
    ),
