      content: '**Botulinum Toxin Injection Plan**:\n\n**Preparation**:\n- Discuss goals with family: improve heel contact, reduce scissoring, improve gait efficiency\n- Consider switching from onabotulinumtoxinA (Botox) to abobotulinumtoxinA (Dysport) if diminishing response suggests antibody formation (different formulations, partial cross-reactivity)\n- Use ultrasound or electrical stimulation guidance for accurate targeting\n\n**Injection Targets and Doses (using onabotulinumtoxinA)**:\n- **Bilateral medial gastrocnemius**: 4 units/kg per leg (primary equinus driver)\n- **Bilateral lateral gastrocnemius**: 2 units/kg per leg\n- **Bilateral hip adductors (adductor longus)**: 2 units/kg per leg (for scissoring)\n- **Bilateral medial hamstrings (semitendinosus)**: 2 units/kg per leg (for crouch and hamstring tightness)\n- Total dose: approximately 20 units/kg body weight per session (360 units at 18 kg body weight), within safe limits (maximum 20 units/kg or 400 units total, whichever is less)\n- Inject under sedation (procedural sedation or nitrous oxide) given the patient\'s age and number of injection sites\n\n**Serial Casting Protocol**:\n- Begin 10-14 days after botulinum toxin injections (when toxin effect is maximal)\n- Apply short-stretch fiberglass casts to bilateral lower legs in maximum tolerated dorsiflexion\n- Change casts weekly for 4-6 weeks, progressively increasing dorsiflexion\n- Goal: achieve at least 10 degrees of passive dorsiflexion with knee extended\n- Monitor skin integrity at each cast change\n- Patient may continue to walk in casts (fiberglass walking casts with rocker bottoms)\n\n**Post-Casting AFO Prescription**:\n- Fabricate new bilateral solid AFOs (or hinged AFOs with plantarflexion stop) immediately after final cast removal to maintain gains\n- Custom-molded, full-length footplate, anterior trim line allowing adequate dorsiflexion\n- Review shoe fit and ensure AFOs are compatible with school shoes\n\n**Ongoing Spasticity Management Strategy**:\n- **PT optimization**: Transition from maintenance PT to goal-directed, task-specific training. Strengthen hip extensors, knee extensors, and ankle dorsiflexors. Work on single-limb stance and dynamic balance.\n- **Home exercise program**: Daily stretching of gastrocnemius, hamstrings, and hip flexors. Standing frame program (45-60 minutes daily for hip development and stretching)\n- **Botulinum toxin cycles**: Every 4-6 months as needed. Track response systematically with GMFM scores and gait parameters.\n- **Annual hip surveillance**: AP pelvis radiograph until skeletal maturity\n- **SDR consideration**: Continue family education. Arrange visit to SDR center for pre-surgical evaluation. Decision is not urgent — window extends to age 8-10.\n- **Orthopedic surgery planning**: If contractures progress despite botulinum toxin and casting, SEMLS (gastrocnemius recession, possible hamstring lengthening, femoral derotation) may be needed. Typically performed around age 7-10 based on gait lab data.\n\n**Monitoring and Follow-up**:\n- Spasticity clinic visits every 4-6 months\n- GMFM-66 assessment every 6-12 months\n- Gait analysis annually or before any surgical intervention\n- Growth monitoring with CP-specific growth charts\n- Bone health assessment if fracture history or prolonged non-weight-bearing',
