    const Section(
      title: '+ Discuss the systems-based issues in managing a child with CP, including insurance, school-based services, Medicaid waivers, and transition planning.',
      content: '**Insurance and Coverage Issues**:\n\n**Botulinum Toxin**:\n- Most private insurers and Medicaid cover botulinum toxin for spasticity in CP\n- Prior authorization typically requires documentation of spasticity severity, functional limitation, and failed conservative treatment\n- Some insurers limit the number of injection sessions per year (typically 3-4)\n- Appeal process: physician letter of medical necessity, peer-to-peer review, citing American Academy of Neurology/American Academy of PM&R guidelines\n\n**DME (Durable Medical Equipment)**:\n- Custom AFOs: Covered under most plans with a prescription and documentation of medical necessity. Replacement every 12-18 months in growing children.\n- Manual wheelchair: Covered for community mobility in GMFCS III. Requires prescription, PT evaluation for fitting, and letter of medical necessity.\n- Standing frame: Often denied as "not medically necessary." Appeal with evidence for hip development, bone density, bowel function, and spasticity management. Cite Paleg et al. systematic review supporting standing programs in CP.\n- Gait trainer/posterior walker: Covered as DME. Replacement as the child grows.\n\n**Medicaid Waivers (Katie Beckett / TEFRA)**:\n- Children with significant disabilities may qualify for Medicaid regardless of parental income through home and community-based waivers\n- Covers services not typically covered by private insurance: extended therapy, respite care, personal care assistants, home modifications\n- Wait lists can be years long depending on the state\n- Application requires documentation of functional limitations and care needs\n\n**SDR Surgery Coverage**:\n- Increasingly covered by major insurers for appropriate candidates\n- Requires comprehensive pre-surgical evaluation including gait lab, psychological assessment, and neurosurgical consultation\n- Some families travel to specialized SDR centers (St. Louis, Alder Hey, etc.), which may be out-of-network\n- Out-of-network appeals may require documentation that the service is not available in-network at the required quality level\n\n**School-Based Therapy vs. Outpatient Therapy**:\n- **School-based PT/OT**: Provided under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA). Goals are educationally relevant (accessing classroom, navigating school, writing). Frequency is often limited (1-2x per week for 30 minutes).\n- **Outpatient PT/OT**: Medically prescribed. Goals are functionally and medically relevant (gait improvement, contracture prevention, post-surgical rehabilitation). Can be more intensive.\n- **These are complementary, not duplicative.** The child should receive both. Document different goals for each to justify coverage.\n- Common insurance denial: "Patient is already receiving PT at school." Appeal: School-based and medical PT have different mandates, goals, and legal frameworks.\n\n**Transition Planning (Even at Age 5, Begin Thinking Ahead)**:\n- Discuss long-term trajectory: GMFCS III patients who walk at age 5 may or may not maintain ambulation into adulthood. Approximately 50% of GMFCS III individuals use a wheelchair as their primary mobility in adulthood.\n- Begin discussing the concept of mobility as a spectrum (walking is not inherently superior to wheeled mobility if it is inefficient and exhausting)\n- Transition to adult care will occur at age 18-21 — plan for transferring medical records, identifying adult CP-experienced physicians, and addressing guardianship if cognitively impaired (not an issue in this patient)\n\n**Family Resources**:\n- United Cerebral Palsy (UCP) — local chapters provide family support, advocacy, and resources\n- Shriners Hospitals for Children — free orthopedic and rehabilitation care for children with CP\n- Easter Seals — therapy services, respite care, adaptive recreation\n- CP Research Network — connect families with clinical trials and research opportunities',
