    const Section(
      title: '+ Mediate the parental conflict about SDR while maintaining both parents\' trust and ensuring shared decision-making.',
      content: '**Step 1 — Acknowledge Both Perspectives as Valid**:\n"I want to thank both of you for being so invested in your son\'s care. The fact that you are both passionate about this shows how much you love him. And I want to be honest with you — you are both right, in a sense. There is strong evidence that SDR can improve outcomes in the right candidate, and there are real risks to any surgery in a five-year-old. These are not contradictory positions. They are two sides of the same careful decision."\n\n**Step 2 — Normalize the Disagreement**:\n"I see families struggle with this exact decision regularly, and it would be unusual if you both felt the same way about it. Different parents bring different perspectives, and that is actually healthy for the decision-making process. You are checking each other\'s reasoning."\n\n**Step 3 — Address the Father\'s Perspective with Nuance**:\n"[Father\'s name], I respect the research you have done, and you are right that the evidence for SDR in well-selected candidates is strong. There are studies showing sustained improvement in gait and spasticity reduction 20 years after the procedure. However, I want to make sure we are looking at the complete picture. SDR works best when spasticity is the primary driver of gait difficulty and there are minimal fixed contractures. Right now, your son has some contracture component developing at his ankles that SDR alone will not address. We need the gait lab data to tell us exactly how much of his walking difficulty is spasticity versus contracture versus bony alignment. If the gait lab shows that spasticity is dominant, SDR becomes a stronger option. If contractures and bony issues are significant, orthopedic surgery may be needed with or without SDR."\n\n**Step 4 — Address the Mother\'s Fear with Specific Information**:\n"[Mother\'s name], your concerns are absolutely valid and shared by many parents. Let me be specific about the risks so we are working with real numbers rather than fear of the unknown. The most common complications are transient: temporary bladder dysfunction in about 10-20% that usually resolves within weeks, temporary sensory changes, and temporary increased weakness during recovery. Serious permanent complications are rare — less than 1-2% in experienced centers. The biggest challenge is the rehabilitation commitment: 6-12 months of intensive therapy, 3-5 times per week. It is a marathon, and the whole family has to be ready for it."\n\n**Step 5 — Provide a Shared Decision-Making Framework**:\n"Here is what I recommend as a path forward that respects both of your positions. First, we proceed with the botulinum toxin and serial casting trial — this addresses the immediate contracture problem and buys us time. Second, we get the instrumented gait analysis — this gives us objective data that takes the decision out of opinion and into evidence. Third, we schedule a consultation with the neurosurgery SDR team — you can meet the surgeon, ask questions, and hear their assessment without committing to anything. After all of that, we sit down together, review the data, and make a decision as a team. There is no rush. The window for SDR extends to age 8-10. Your son is five. We have time to get this right."\n\n**Step 6 — Center the Child**:\n"One more thing. Your son is five, and while he cannot make this decision, his experience matters. He is going to do better with whatever treatment his parents are aligned on. If one of you feels coerced into a decision you are not comfortable with, that stress will affect the whole family and his recovery. The goal is for both of you to feel confident, not for one person to win and the other to lose."\n\n**Key Principles**:\n- Do not take sides — you are a facilitator, not an arbiter\n- Use data (gait lab) to depersonalize the decision\n- Provide specific risk numbers rather than vague reassurances\n- Create a sequential plan that addresses both parents\' needs (caution and action)\n- Affirm that taking time to decide is not the same as doing nothing',
